# 30. Data Governance Decisions

The following principles were applied throughout the rent-cleaning phase.

## Preserve Raw Data

No original CMHC BigQuery source table was edited.

All cleaning was performed using:

```text
SELECT queries
Pandas DataFrames
validation checks
new cleaned outputs
```

---

## Do Not Convert Missing Data to Zero

A missing rent estimate does not represent free or zero-cost housing.

Null values were treated as unavailable observations.

Only published numerical observations were included in the final `rent_clean` analytical table.

---

## Preserve Quality Information

CMHC reliability flags were not discarded.

Both:

```text
quality_flag
quality_level
```

are retained in the cleaned table.

---

## Preserve Lineage

Every observation retains:

```text
source_table
```

so the original BigQuery source can be identified if an observation requires investigation later.

---

## Separate Geography From Indicator Availability

The 25-municipality reference table remains separate from indicator fact tables.

This ensures that geographic scope is not determined by whether CMHC happened to publish a particular estimate.

---

# 31. CMHC Survey Coverage Limitation

The CMHC Rental Market Survey does not represent every rental unit in the housing market.

CMHC states that the survey is conducted in October and targets privately initiated rental structures containing at least three rental units that have been on the market for at least three months. Social and affordable housing structures or units are excluded from the Rental Market Survey frame.

Therefore, the project's CMHC rent estimates should not be interpreted as representing every possible rental arrangement, such as every condominium rental or secondary-market unit.

This limitation will be documented in the final methodology and Power BI dashboard.

