# Conclusion

The second phase of the GTA Housing Affordability project completed the end-to-end cleaning and validation of the historical CMHC rent data.

The process identified important source limitations that would have been hidden if all municipality tables had simply been concatenated without inspection.

Three major availability exceptions were identified: King does not contain a comparable historical rent table, while East Gwillimbury and Vaughan contain historical reference periods but no usable published rent estimates.

The investigation also demonstrated that the 2025 Toronto CMA CSD table does not represent the full geographic scope of the project. Municipality-specific historical tables provided broader GTA coverage and reproduced the same 2025 values wherever the two sources overlapped.

As a result, the historical municipality tables were selected as the primary rent source.

The final cleaned dataset contains:

```text
2,952 published CMHC rent observations
22 municipalities with at least some historical rent data
17 municipalities with a published 2025 two-bedroom estimate
18 municipalities with a published 2025 Total-rent estimate
0 analytical duplicates
0 missing analytical rent values
0 zero or negative rent values
```

The final structure preserves CSD identifiers, municipality and regional classifications, reference years, bedroom categories, CMHC reliability classifications, and source-table lineage.

The historical rent-cleaning phase is therefore complete.

The immediate next action is to publish and verify `rent_clean` and `municipality_reference` in the separate `cleaned_cmhc` BigQuery layer. Once that validation is complete, the project will proceed to the historical vacancy-cleaning phase using the same controlled cleaning and validation framework.
